import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>2.2b — String Methods: Apply and Build</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Yesterday you explored String methods by experimenting. Today you apply them
        to solve problems. You'll write methods that take Strings in and return something
        useful out — exactly what RPG systems do behind the scenes.
    </p>

    <h3>Recap: The Methods You'll Need</h3>

    <p>Open the <a href="../docs/unit-02/string.html">String docs</a> and have them ready. You'll use:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>length()</code> — how many characters</li>
        <li><code>substring(begin, end)</code> — extract a piece</li>
        <li><code>indexOf(String)</code> — find where something starts</li>
        <li><code>equals(Object)</code> — compare contents</li>
        <li><code>toUpperCase()</code> / <code>toLowerCase()</code> — case conversion</li>
        <li><code>trim()</code> — strip leading/trailing whitespace</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <h3>Trap Review: == vs equals()</h3>
        <p>Before you start, make sure you can answer this: why does <code>"sword" == "sword"</code> sometimes work but <code>a.equals(b)</code> always works? If you can't explain it, re-read the <a href="../docs/unit-02/objects-and-classes.html">Objects &amp; Classes docs</a> — look for the section on reference comparison.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Build 1: Weapon Name Formatter</h3>

    <p>Create <code>WeaponName.java</code>. Write a method called <code>formatWeapon</code> that takes a raw weapon name and returns it properly formatted:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Trim whitespace from both ends</li>
        <li>Convert to lowercase</li>
        <li>Capitalize the first letter</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>// input → output examples (test these):
"sWoRD  "   → "Sword"
"   aXE"   → "Axe"
"BOW"       → "Bow"</code></pre>

    <p>
        You'll need a way to get the first letter, convert it to uppercase, and combine
        it with the rest of the word. The String docs show you how. This is a two-step
        process — don't try to do it in one line.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens if your method receives an empty string <code>""</code>? Does it crash? Test it. If it does, what check could you add?</li>
            <li>What happens if it receives <code>null</code>? Try it. This is a different kind of error. What's it called?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h3>Build 2: Email Extractor</h3>

    <p>Create <code>EmailExtractor.java</code>. In RPGs, items often have metadata embedded in strings. Given a string like <code>"player@dungeon"</code>, extract:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>The username — everything before the '@'</li>
        <li>The domain — everything after the '@'</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>// input → output:
"warrior@dungeon"  → username: "warrior", domain: "dungeon"
"mage@castle.org"  → username: "mage",    domain: "castle.org"</code></pre>

    <p>
        You'll need <code>indexOf("@")</code> to find the split point, then
        <code>substring</code> to extract both halves. The String docs have
        examples of exactly this pattern.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: What If There's No '@'?</h3>
        <p>
            Run your EmailExtractor with <code>"justaname"</code> — a string with no '@'.
            What happens? What does indexOf return when it can't find the character —
            and why does that break substring?
        </p>
        <p>
            Fix it: if the '@' isn't found, return the original string as the username
            and <code>""</code> as the domain. You'll need an if statement. If you're not
            sure how, open the
            <a href="../docs/unit-03/boolean-logic.html">Boolean Expressions docs</a>
            for a refresher.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Combine substring, indexOf, and trim to parse structured strings</li>
            <li>Handle edge cases: empty strings, missing delimiters</li>
            <li>Use equals() (not ==) to compare String contents</li>
            <li>Explain what happens when you call a method on a null reference</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/string.html">The String Class</a> — full method reference</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/objects-and-classes.html">Objects &amp; Classes</a> — reference comparison</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/boolean-logic.html">Boolean Expressions</a> — if statements</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
